        "finalMessage": "I fixed both issues by putting the object keys in sorted order (`label`, `level`, `message`) in both `JSON.stringify` calls in `json.ts`.\n\nSorting the keys alone would have changed the log output: `JSON.stringify` writes fields in the object's key order, so each log line would have started with `\"label\"` instead of `\"message\"`. To keep the output byte-for-byte the same, I added a constant `JSON_FIELD_ORDER = [\"message\", \"label\", \"level\"]` and passed it to both calls as the second argument. When that argument is an array, `JSON.stringify` writes the listed fields in the array's order, whatever order the object's keys are in. It can also drop fields, but nothing is lost here: all three fields are listed, and their values are only strings or `null`.\n\nI couldn't run the quick Node check comparing old and new output because the command wasn't approved, so this hasn't been tested or re-linted here.",
